Large dialog:

```text
32px
```

---

# 45 — DRAWERS

Desktop:

```text
Small  = 320px
Medium = 400px
Large  = 480px
XL     = 640px
```

Mobile:

```text
width = 100%
```

unless a contextual sheet is more appropriate.

---

# 46 — BOTTOM SHEETS

Mobile:

```text
width = 100%
max-height = 90vh
```

Preferred top radius:

```text
24px
```

Drag handle:

```text
width  = 36px
height = 4px
```

Handle radius:

```text
4px
```

---

# 47 — TOOLTIPS

Default:

```text
font = 12px
line-height = 16px
padding = 6px 8px
radius = 8px
```

Maximum width:

```text
280px
```

Delay:

```text
initial = 400–500ms
```

Don't use tooltips for essential information.

---

# 48 — BADGES / CHIPS

Small badge:

```text
height = 20px
padding = 4px 8px
font = 11–12px
```

Standard chip:

```text
height = 28px
padding = 6px 10px
```

Large chip:

```text
height = 32px
padding = 8px 12px
```

---

# 49 — AVATARS

```text
XS = 24px
SM = 32px
MD = 40px
LG = 48px
XL = 64px
2XL = 80px
3XL = 120px
```

Avatar group overlap:

```text
20–30%
```

Never sacrifice recognition for excessive overlap.

---

# 50 — SKELETONS

Skeleton radius should match the actual content component.

Animation:

```text
duration = 1200–1800ms
```

Reduced motion:

```text
static
```

Skeletons should approximately represent the final content geometry rather than generic gray rectangles.

---

# 51 — LOADING STATES

Use:

```text
<300ms
→ no loading indicator

300–1000ms
→ subtle inline feedback when needed

>1000ms
→ visible progress/loading

long operation
→ progress + ability to continue/cancel where appropriate
```

Never show a spinner for a 100ms operation.

---

# 52 — RESPONSIVE TYPE

Desktop display values may scale down approximately:

```text
Desktop → Tablet = -8px
Tablet → Mobile = -8px
```

But do not blindly scale every text style.

Example:

```text
Display 1
Desktop = 64px
Tablet  = 56px
Mobile  = 44px
```

Large title:

```text
Desktop = 40px
Tablet  = 36px
Mobile  = 32px
```

---

# 53 — MOBILE RULE

Mobile is **not desktop compressed to 375px**.

At mobile widths:

* reduce simultaneous actions
* prioritize primary actions
* collapse secondary actions
* transform tables
* stack complex forms
* convert sidebars to sheets
* convert desktop menus to sheets/popovers
* increase touch affordance
* preserve readable text
* reduce decorative elements

---

# 54 — DESKTOP RULE

Desktop should exploit additional space.

Do not simply center everything.

Use:

* multi-column layouts
* persistent navigation
* contextual panels
* keyboard shortcuts
* hover interactions
* dense tables
* split views
* resizable panels
* multi-pane workflows

---

# 55 — TABLET RULE

Tablet should be treated as a **distinct interaction environment**.

Support:

* touch
* stylus
* optional keyboard
* intermediate layouts
* collapsible navigation
* adaptive panels
* two-column forms
* touch-friendly data manipulation

---

# 56 — ICONOGRAPHY RULE

Every icon must have:

```text
meaning
consistent stroke/weight
optical alignment
accessible label when needed
```

Use one coherent icon family.

Do not mix:

```text
outline icons
filled icons
3D icons
emoji
random SVG packs
```

within the same visual context.

---

# 57 — BUTTON HIERARCHY

Maximum recommended prominent actions in a local action group:

```text
1 primary
1 secondary
0–2 tertiary
```

If a toolbar has 12 equal-looking primary buttons, the hierarchy is broken.

---

# 58 — CARDS

Cards should not be used automatically.

Use a card when:

* content is independently meaningful
* content needs grouping
* content needs interaction
* content needs visual separation

Do not put every piece of information inside a rounded card.

---

# 59 — INFORMATION HIERARCHY

Every screen should have:

```text
Level 1 = primary purpose
Level 2 = primary action/content
Level 3 = supporting information
Level 4 = metadata
Level 5 = tertiary/system information
```

Visual weight must decrease accordingly.

---

# 60 — VISUAL ATTENTION

Default visual hierarchy:

```text
Primary action/content       = 100%
Secondary                    = 70%
Supporting                   = 50%
Metadata                     = 40%
Disabled                     = 30–40%
```

These are **relative emphasis targets**, not literal opacity values.

---

# 61 — INTERACTION FEEDBACK

Default state changes should occur within:

```text
80–160ms
```

Pressed feedback:

```text
immediate
```

The UI must respond to a press within the same frame whenever technically possible.

---

# 62 — HOVER

Hover is optional.

Never make critical functionality dependent on hover.

Hover behavior is primarily for:

* desktop pointer environments
* previews
* secondary information
* discoverability

Touch devices should not simulate hover as a required interaction.

---

# 63 — PRESS FEEDBACK

Press states should use one or more:

```text
slight scale reduction
surface change
opacity change
shadow reduction
color shift
icon movement
```

Typical scale:

```text
0.97–0.99
```

Avoid dramatic scaling.

---

# 64 — ACCESSIBILITY

Every component must support:

```text
keyboard
screen reader
focus
contrast
zoom
dynamic type
reduced motion
reduced transparency
high contrast
RTL
localization
touch
stylus
```

---

# 65 — KEYBOARD

Standard:

```text
Tab
Shift + Tab
Enter
Space
Escape
Arrow keys
Home
End
Page Up
Page Down
```

Components must follow platform-appropriate keyboard semantics.

Never invent keyboard behavior unnecessarily.

---

# 66 — FOCUS

Focus must be:

* visible
* high contrast
* consistent
* non-destructive
* keyboard-friendly

Default:

```text
2px ring
2px offset
```

---

# 67 — RTL

Every component must support RTL without creating a separate component.

Automatically mirror:

* directional icons
* padding
* alignment
* navigation
* menus
* arrows
* breadcrumbs

Do **not** blindly mirror non-directional icons.

---

# 68 — LOCALIZATION

Design for at least:

```text
+30% text expansion
```

without breaking ordinary layouts.

Critical interfaces should tolerate:

```text
+50–100%
```

where practical.

---

# 69 — NUMBER FORMATTING

Never hardcode assumptions about:

```text
1,000.00
$1,000
10/12/2026
```

Use locale-aware formatting.

Support:

* currencies
* percentages
* dates
* times
* decimals
* compact numbers

---

# 70 — DATE/TIME

Support:

```text
12/24-hour formats
locale-specific date order
timezone
relative time
absolute time
calendar systems where required
```

Never assume one global date format.

---

# 71 — ERROR UX

Error hierarchy:

```text
Field-level error
↓
Section-level error
↓
Page-level error
↓
Global system error
```

Always show the error as close as practical to its source.

---

# 72 — EMPTY UX

Every meaningful data component must support:

```text
loading
empty
populated
partial
error
permission denied
offline
```

Do not leave blank spaces when data isn't available.

---

# 73 — PERMISSION STATES

Enterprise components should distinguish:

```text
not authenticated
not authorized
read-only
limited access
full access
```

Do not simply hide everything without explaining why appropriate.

---

# 74 — PERFORMANCE RULES

Components should:

* avoid unnecessary rerenders
* lazy-load heavy functionality
* virtualize very large lists
* avoid unnecessary blur
* avoid excessive DOM depth
* respect reduced motion
* avoid continuous animation
* optimize images
* avoid expensive shadows/materials unnecessarily

---

# 75 — ANIMATION PERFORMANCE

Prefer GPU-friendly properties:

```text
transform
opacity
```

Avoid animating layout-heavy properties continuously:

```text
width
height
top
left
margin
```

unless required.

---

# 76 — RESPONSIVE IMAGES

Images should support:

```text
object-fit
aspect-ratio
responsive loading
lazy loading
high-density displays
```

Canonical aspect ratios:

```text
1:1
4:3
3:2
16:9
21:9
```

---

# 77 — ASPECT RATIOS

Use semantic aspect tokens rather than arbitrary dimensions.

Examples:

```text
avatar     = 1:1
thumbnail  = 4:3
video      = 16:9
hero       = 16:9 / adaptive
portrait   = 3:4
```

---

# 78 — DESIGN TOKEN ARCHITECTURE

Every visual property must ultimately resolve through:

```text
Primitive Token
      ↓
Semantic Token
      ↓
Component Token
      ↓
Rendered Component
```

Example:

```text
primitive.color.blue.500
        ↓
semantic.color.accent.primary
        ↓
component.button.primary.background
        ↓
Button
```

---

# 79 — NO MAGIC VALUES

Do not write:

```text
margin: 17px
border-radius: 13px
color: #123456
box-shadow: 0 7px 21px...
```

inside components.

Use:

```text
space-4
radius-5
color-accent-primary
elevation-2
```

unless a component-specific token is intentionally introduced.

---

# 80 — COMPONENT TOKEN OVERRIDES

Components may define specialized tokens:

```text
button.height
button.padding
button.radius
button.icon-size
button.gap
button.motion
```

But component tokens must ultimately derive from global primitives.

---

# 81 — THEMING

Every component must support:

```text
Light
Dark
High Contrast
Custom Brand
```

without rewriting component structure.

---

# 82 — BRANDING

Allow customization of:

```text
accent color
logo
font
radius personality
density
surface intensity
motion intensity
```

But prevent customization from destroying accessibility or system consistency.

---

# 83 — DENSITY PERSONALITY

Provide:

```text
Compact
Comfortable
Spacious
```

Default:

```text
Comfortable
```

Enterprise users can choose Compact.

Consumer/touch-heavy experiences can choose Spacious.

---

# 84 — SHADOW PERSONALITY

Three modes:

```text
Minimal
Balanced
Expressive
```

Default:

```text
Minimal
```

The system should never become a generic "card + shadow" UI.

---

# 85 — RADIUS PERSONALITY

Three optional global personalities:

```text
Sharp
Balanced
Soft
```

Default:

```text
Balanced
```

But component semantics still control appropriate radius.

---

# 86 — MOTION PERSONALITY

```text
Reduced
Balanced
Expressive
```

Default:

```text
Balanced
```

User accessibility settings always override this.

---

# 87 — VISUAL MATERIAL PERSONALITY

```text
Solid
Subtle Material
Expressive Material
```

Default:

```text
Subtle Material
```

Avoid making the entire application translucent.

---

# 88 — SCREEN EDGE SAFETY

Mobile interfaces must account for:

```text
top safe area
bottom safe area
left safe area
right safe area
```

especially for:

* bottom navigation
* sheets
* full-screen dialogs
* floating buttons
* media
* immersive interfaces

---

# 89 — MOBILE BOTTOM SAFE AREA

For bottom controls:

```text
minimum visual padding = 16px
safe-area inset = additional
```

Never place critical controls directly against the physical screen edge.

---

# 90 — DESKTOP WINDOW SAFETY

Large screens must avoid excessively stretched content.

For a 4K display:

```text
content max-width ≠ 3840px
```

Use:

```text
max-width
multi-column layout
side panels
whitespace
```

to preserve hierarchy.

---

# 91 — COMPONENT ANATOMY STANDARD

Every future component specification must document:

```text
1. Purpose
2. Anatomy
3. Variants
4. Sizes
5. States
6. Content rules
7. Spacing
8. Typography
9. Color
10. Radius
11. Elevation
12. Icons
13. Interaction
14. Motion
15. Responsive behavior
16. Accessibility
17. Keyboard behavior
18. Touch behavior
19. RTL
20. Localization
21. Loading
22. Empty
23. Error
24. Disabled
25. Permission
26. API
27. Events
28. Slots
29. Composition
30. Edge cases
31. Performance
```

---

# 92 — COMPONENT QUALITY STANDARD

A component is **not complete** until all of these work:

```text
Phone
Tablet
Desktop
Large Desktop

Light
Dark
High Contrast

Mouse
Keyboard
Touch
Stylus

Loading
Empty
Error
Success
Disabled

LTR
RTL

Short text
Long text
Translated text

Normal motion
Reduced motion
```

---

# 93 — DESIGN ANTI-PATTERNS

Never allow:

```text
Excessive gradients
Excessive glass
Excessive rounded cards
Excessive shadows
Random colors
Random spacing
Random icon styles
Tiny touch targets
Invisible focus
Hover-only actions
Color-only status
Overly dense mobile UI
Desktop UI squeezed into mobile
Infinite scrolling without alternatives
Unnecessary animation
Decorative UI without purpose
```

---

# 94 — APPLE-CLASS DETAIL RULE

Every component must receive a final **optical refinement pass**.

Check:

```text
1px alignment
icon baseline
text baseline
visual center
padding symmetry
corner transitions
shadow softness
color contrast
animation timing
touch target
focus position
```

Do not rely solely on mathematically correct CSS.

---

# 95 — 3-LEVEL VISUAL HIERARCHY

Every component should have:

```text
Primary
Secondary
Tertiary
```

Never allow every element to compete equally for attention.

---

# 96 — PRIMARY ACTION RULE

A local UI context should normally contain:

```text
1 primary action
```

If multiple actions are equally important, use:

```text
secondary actions
overflow menu
segmented controls
workflow structure
```

rather than making everything primary.

---

# 97 — ICON + TEXT RULE

Use icon + text when the icon alone is ambiguous.

Use icon-only when:

```text
meaning is universally recognizable
space is constrained
tooltip/accessibility label exists
```

Never force users to memorize mysterious icons.

---

# 98 — FINAL VISUAL TARGET

Every component should satisfy this visual equation:

```text
Beauty
=
Typography
+
Spacing
+
Hierarchy
+
Proportion
+
Material
+
Motion
+
Interaction
+
Consistency
```

Not:

```text
Beauty
=
Gradients
+
Shadows
+
Rounded corners
+
Animations
```

---

# 99 — NON-NEGOTIABLE COMPONENT RULE

Every one of the future **3,000 components** must inherit this foundation.

Therefore:

**Do not repeat these rules inside individual component prompts.**

Each component prompt should only specify what is unique to that component.

For example:

```text
GLOBAL FOUNDATION
        ↓
Button Component
        ↓
Button-specific anatomy
Button-specific variants
Button-specific states
Button-specific behavior
```

Not:

```text
Button prompt
+ duplicate colors
+ duplicate spacing
+ duplicate typography
+ duplicate responsive rules
+ duplicate accessibility rules
```

---

# 100 — MASTER PROMPT FOR YOUR DEVKIT GENERATOR

Use this as the **system/master prompt** for generating every future component:

```text
You are designing a production-grade component for a world-class original design system.

The DevKit Core Design Constitution is the single source of truth for all visual, interaction, responsive, accessibility, motion, spacing, typography, color, radius, elevation, material, density, and layout decisions.

The visual quality target is Apple-class craftsmanship without copying Apple's proprietary interface, assets, exact layouts, or branded implementation.

The component must feel exceptionally refined, calm, premium, precise, fluid, intelligent, and beautiful.

Use the exact global design tokens defined by the Constitution. Never invent arbitrary spacing, colors, typography, radius, shadows, elevations, animation durations, breakpoints, or control dimensions.

The canonical spatial system is based on a 4px base unit and an 8px primary rhythm.

Use the canonical responsive tiers:
Compact 0–479px,
Mobile 480–767px,
Tablet 768–1023px,
Desktop 1024–1279px,
Large Desktop 1280–1535px,
XL Desktop 1536–1919px,
Ultra Wide 1920px+.

The component must be mobile-first but must not simply scale between devices. It must intelligently transform its information architecture, layout, density, interaction model, and available actions according to available space and input method.

Support Light, Dark, High Contrast, Custom Brand, Reduced Motion, Reduced Transparency, Compact Density, Comfortable Density, Spacious Density, LTR, RTL, localization, dynamic text scaling, keyboard, mouse, touch, stylus, and screen readers.

Use semantic color tokens rather than literal colors.

Use the global typography scale rather than arbitrary font sizes.

Use the global spacing scale rather than arbitrary pixel values.

Use semantic radius tokens rather than arbitrary border-radius values.

Use semantic elevation tokens rather than arbitrary shadows.

Use semantic motion tokens rather than arbitrary animation timings.

The component must have a clear visual hierarchy with one primary purpose and appropriately subordinate secondary actions.

Do not use visual decoration unless it improves comprehension, hierarchy, interaction, or emotional quality.

Do not use excessive cards, borders, shadows, gradients, glass effects, badges, icons, or animation.

Use whitespace, typography, tonal contrast, proportion, and surface hierarchy as the primary tools for visual refinement.

Every interactive element must have appropriate:
default,
hover where applicable,
focus,
pressed,
selected,
disabled,
loading,
success,
warning,
error
states.

Every component must have appropriate:
loading,
empty,
error,
overflow,
long-content,
permission,
localization,
accessibility,
and responsive behaviors where relevant.

All interactive targets must meet the global touch-target requirements.

All keyboard interaction must follow appropriate semantic behavior.

All focus states must remain visible.

Color must never be the sole method of communicating state.

Motion must communicate spatial or state relationships and must respect reduced-motion preferences.

Materials must remain readable and must provide non-material fallbacks.

The component must support optical alignment, not merely mathematical alignment.

The component must be composable with other DevKit components.

Do not create separate components for mobile, tablet, desktop, light mode, dark mode, or accessibility modes. These are states, variants, themes, or responsive behaviors of the same component.

Do not duplicate an existing component under another name merely to increase the component count.

Do not split obvious sub-elements into independent components unless they have meaningful standalone reuse.

The component must be optimized for production performance and should avoid unnecessary DOM complexity, rendering, continuous animation, expensive effects, and unnecessary computation.

Before finalizing the component, perform a complete quality review across:

visual hierarchy,
spacing,
typography,
color,
proportion,
optical alignment,
interaction,
motion,
responsive behavior,
accessibility,
keyboard,
touch,
stylus,
RTL,
localization,
loading,
empty,
error,
long content,
performance,
and theme compatibility.

The final result must look and behave like it belongs to one coherent world-class design system.

The standard is not "functional."

The standard is:
beautiful,
precise,
accessible,
responsive,
intuitive,
fast,
consistent,
and exceptionally refined.
```